{
  "$comment": "#EXP-1950: SIGNED TREE KEY WRAPS, the one byte format every platform signs and checks. Replayed by the TS reference packages/domain-contract/src/wrap-signature.ts and by Rust enet-core wrap_sig.rs (the crate every platform's crypto goes through; Swift, Kotlin and the browser follow through its bindings). CONTRACT ONLY: no platform signs or checks a wrap yet, and how an install comes to be PINNED is not decided here. WHY: a wrap (`session_keys.wrapped`, fixtures/sealed-content.json `wrap`) is a sealed box to an install's public key, which the server stores; an install that keeps any wrap that opens can be handed a key the server made itself. SIGNER: the install that made the wrap signs it with its eNet identity, the ed25519 key whose public half is `installs.endpoint_id` (fixtures/install-register.json; `signer.identity` here is that fixture's identity). MESSAGE (186 bytes, `layout`): `label` = the ASCII of `label` and one 0x00; `rootId` = the root UUID's 16 bytes (so its casing never matters); `epoch` = u32 big endian; `treePublic` = the 32 bytes of that epoch's X25519 public key; `recipient` = the recipient install's ed25519 public key (its endpoint id, never the row id: the server assigns row ids); `wrapped` = the 81 bytes of the wrap. SIGNATURE = ed25519 (RFC 8032, deterministic, so `vector` is byte-exact) over the message, 64 bytes; it travels as base64url WITHOUT padding (86 chars), endpoint ids as lowercase hex, secrets here as lowercase hex. VERIFICATION IS STRICT (iroh `verify_strict`): canonical S, a low-order signer key or R is refused. ACCEPTING A WRAP (`accepted`, `refused`; `acceptWrap`): the recipient (`recipient`: its identity and the X25519 secret derived from it, fixtures/push-seal.json `derive`) builds the message ITSELF from the root and epoch it asked for, the public key of the secret it just unwrapped, its own endpoint id and the wrapped bytes. Checks in this order, first failure wins: `malformed` (a field the wire does not allow; a signature without its signer or the reverse) > `unsigned` (no signature and `required`) > `unpinned` (the signer is not in `pins`) > `unopened` (the wrap does not open with the install secret) > `signature` (not the signer's signature over this message). A wrap that CARRIES a signature must pass with it also where `required` is false: `unsigned` is only ever the status of a wrap with no signature at all. So a wrong signer, recipient, root, epoch or tree public key and a re-sealed wrap all read `signature`. VOUCH (`vouchCases`; `acceptVouch`): an install that holds no secret of (root, epoch) may seal to a tree public key only when a pinned install's signed wrap of that epoch, made for ANOTHER install, names that key; it verifies the same message without opening the wrap (never `unopened`). Each case carries every input, so a replay needs no state.",
